    "Briefly, how do you perform Principal Component Analysis (PCA)? Should I get anxious about its mathematics?": "PCA reduces dimensionality by transforming data to new axes (principal components) that explain variance. Start with raw data in *n* dimensions; center it by subtracting mean values. Compute covariance matrix, then eigenvectors of the matrix—these are principal directions. Sort eigenvectors by eigenvalues (largest = most important). Project your data onto these top eigenvectors to get reduced dimensions. Math-wise? It’s linear algebra heavy (eigenvalues/vectors), but tools like Python’s `sklearn.decomposition.PCA` abstract away some complexity. No need to dread it—it’s just statistics behind clustering!",
